.Dd September 8, 2026
.Dt BOSD 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm bosd
.Nd on-screen display engine
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl n Ar instance
.Fl d
.Nm
.Op Fl n Ar instance
.Ar icon
.Op Ar hold_seconds
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
paints a PNG glyph, scaled to the internal panel and outlined for
legibility, in a centered click-through ARGB32 overlay window.
It is the display engine behind media-key and hotkey feedback: a
warm daemon owns the window, and clients replace the visible glyph
in place, so rapid key repeats never flash.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "-n instance"
.It Fl d
Run as the display daemon for
.Ar instance ,
listening on a per-user datagram socket.
.It Fl n Ar instance
Select the OSD channel (default
.Dq Li default ) .
Independent channels own independent windows and sockets.
.El
.Pp
Without
.Fl d ,
.Nm
sends a show request to the running daemon for the channel; if no
daemon is alive, it paints the glyph itself and exits when
.Ar hold_seconds
(default 2.0, clamped to 0.5\(en30) elapse.
.Sh ICON RESOLUTION
An
.Ar icon
containing a slash is used as a path.
A bare name is searched in each directory of the colon-separated
.Ev BOSD_PATH ,
then in the compiled share directory.
A
.Pa .png
suffix is appended when missing.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "BOSD_PATH"
.It Ev BOSD_PATH
Colon-separated icon search path, tried before the compiled default.
.It Ev DISPLAY
X11 display to draw on.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/tmp/bosd.instance.uid.sock" -compact
.It Pa /tmp/bosd. Ns Ar instance . Ns Ar uid Ns Pa .sock
show-request socket
.It Pa /tmp/bosd. Ns Ar instance . Ns Ar uid Ns Pa .pid
daemon pid
.It Pa /usr/local/share/bosd
default icon directory
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Warm the airplane-mode channel at session start:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bosd -n airplane -d &
.Ed
.Pp
Flash the on glyph for 1.5 seconds:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bosd -n airplane airplane-on 1.5
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xrandr 1
